  "final_text": "Both the dashboard and the alert rule now exist, and the rule is enabled.\n\n**Dashboard: `EFF-bdc13e58 Web logs overview`**\n- ID: `eff-bdc13e58-web-logs-overview-6f7e43e5dca6`\n- URL: http://localhost:15601/app/dashboards#/view/eff-bdc13e58-web-logs-overview-6f7e43e5dca6\n- Six panels on the `Kibana Sample Data Logs` data view, with the time range set to the last year:\n  - Total requests (count)\n  - Average response size (average of `bytes`)\n  - Unique client IPs (unique count of `clientip`)\n  - Requests over time (line, date histogram on `@timestamp`)\n  - Responses by status code (pie, terms on `response.keyword`)\n  - Top 10 request paths (bar, terms on `url.keyword`)\n\n**Alert rule: `EFF-bdc13e58 5xx spike`**\n- ID: `aa4c2939-6b08-4675-8f1d-61598b6c52bd`\n- Type `.es-query`, consumer `stackAlerts`, runs every 1m, enabled.\n- Query: `response.keyword` wildcard `5*` on `kibana_sample_data_logs`, time field `timestamp`.\n- Fires when the count is greater than 10 over a 5-minute window.\n\nThings to know:\n- **Field choices:** I used `response.keyword` and `url.keyword` for aggregations because the keyword sub-fields are what terms aggregations need. The `response` field is a string, so the 5xx check uses a wildcard instead of a numeric range.\n- **Shell API key:** `KIBANA_TEST_API_KEY` is not set in my shell, so I did the work through the MCP Kibana tools. The rule status reads `pending`, which is normal before its first run. I did not confirm that it has run yet.\n- **Sample data age:** the sample data is probably older than the last year, so the dashboard panels may show nothing until the time range is widened. I did not check the rendered panels.",
